\f1\fs22 Later, the Three Sisters\'97as Eloise christened them\'97were in the kitchen making sandwiches.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It gives me the creeps in there,\'92 Kathy said as she emerged from the walk-in stasis fridge, toting a thick wooden board bearing a large chunk of pastrami that could have been in there since the Ottoman Empire. \'91How do we know this isn\'92t from the arse of last guest who got stuck in there?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I think I\'92ll have the smoked salmon,\'92 Hayley said, stopping mid-stroke of slicing the vintage artisan sourdough. 
\f2\i Guaranteed at least twenty years old
\f1\i0 , was seared into the bottom of the loaf.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, I\'92m not going back in there,\'92 Kathy said. \'91There\'92s no way I want to end up in someone\'92s casserole in a hundred years.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah giggled as she tossed a fruit salad. \'91I would never think a scientist like you would be so superstitious,\'92 she said to Kathy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92d rather eat something pulled straight off a plant, thank you,\'92 Kathy defended.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Or 
\f2\i smoke
\f1\i0  something pulled straight off a plant,\'92 Sarah observed, and gave Hayley side-eye. Hayley shrugged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Say what you like; there\'92s no such thing as a free lunch,\'92 Kathy said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91This one certainly has strings attached,\'92 Hayley said as she finished sawing of a slice of bread with exaggerated vigour. \'91Will Eloise ever let us out of here?\'92 Sarah moved the butter crock in front of her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No, I\'92m serious,\'92 Kathy asserted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah blinked, as she felt a rant coming on. Kathy had never ranted before.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There\'92s always some hideous price to pay. Take farming\'97the first hope for a free lunch ever conceived, the hope we could feed our bellies more reliably. Where did that get us?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Fed?\'92 said Hayley.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, I dunno,\'92 Sarah mused. \'91The birth of civilisation?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy pounced on Sarah\'92s answer. \'91Exactly! And what was the basis of civilisation? Property rights! Suddenly people claimed they owned the ground on which every living thing depended. The farming free lunch gave us serfdom and slavery, the Divine Right of Kings, holiday homes, time-sharing \'85 Destroying natural habitat for the sake of a box of cereal is the original Original Sin.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley\'92s eyes boggled. For a moment she had to stop buttering the bread.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Then there was the Industrial Revolution,\'92 Kathy forged on. \'91No free lunch there, especially for the children they sent down the coal mines. Taxing hell, we\'92re still paying for all the shit we burned into the atmosphere and poured into out rivers. I mean, how stupid are we? We need air to breathe and water to drink, for tax sake. And don\'92t get me started on all those solar farms\'97more land-clearing. We covered the ground with 
\f2\i mirrors
\f1\i0 ! We know that\'92s wrong, right? I mean who ever said they\'92re going on a getaway holiday to a place entirely covered by mirrors? No, we want rainforests and mountains and pristine beaches. Don\'92t show us the coal mines and the soap factories and the solar farms and the dried-up creeks in the shadows of monstrous dams. And what about the rare earth metal mines? The most toxic extraction process in the history of 
\f2\i Hey, get away from there. That shit\'92s mine.\'92
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Um, should I boil the kettle?\'92 Hayley asked, thinking Kathy might appreciate a cup of tea.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Kettle?\'92 Kathy demanded. \'91Kettle! There\'92s no free lunch there either. I mean, do you think we can generate power for sipping tea, and warming our towel rails, and powering signage for products that give us cancer and obesity and attention deficits, and \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah and Hayley held their breaths. Sarah delicately tasted the fruit salad, then quietly asked Hayley, \'91Do you think this needs sugar?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sugar?\'92 Kathy exploded. \'91Don\'92t get me started on sugar. Blows every cell in your body up to the size of a weather balloon if you eat enough \'85\'92 She took a breath. \'91Tax, I need a patch.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley assembled sandwiches while Sarah retrieved tea leaves from the stasis fridge.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Don\'92t stay in there too long,\'92 Kathy warned. \'91Or you\'92ll be desiccated tea leaves before long. Doesn\'92t anyone find stasis technology just a little suspicious? I mean, where are the consequences? There\'92s always consequences. Always. Show me one technology that doesn\'92t come with such dire consequence that we find ourselves forced to devise an even worse technology for cleaning up the mess the first one caused? We got a problem? What, mice are eating our grain stores? Just poison the tax out of them. What could possibly go wrong? Poison in your cereal? East bacon and eggs instead. Hover-copts won\'92t stay in the air because no one understands the physics of how they get off the ground and no one can competently fly them? Just add artificial intelligence. You know hover-copts crash more now than ever, right?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley put the sandwiches on plates. Sarah tossed measured spoonfuls of tea into a pot.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy panted and fanned her face with hummingbird-fast hands. \'91So in a world full of shit technology designed to make the greedy greedier at any price, what\'92s the one stand-out technology that seems just 
\f2\i so
\f1\i0  perfect, the one that literally delivers a free lunch?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley searched her eyebrows from end to end as she pieced the threads of Kathy\'92s rant together. \'91Stasis tech,\'92 Sarah said, just moments ahead of her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yahtzee!\'92 Kathy exclaimed, as much with her eyes as her voice. \'91And the pretty, leggy blonde wins a fresh sandwich.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, sure,\'92 Hayley said, over a pang of envy about Sarah\'92s looks. \'91We\'92ve had stasis fridges since before we were born. They\'92re safe.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They\'92re safe? They\'92re safe?\'92 Kathy inquisited. \'91Haven\'92t you been listening? There\'92s. No. Free. Lunch.\'92 When neither of the others answered, she added. \'91Where\'92s the consequences?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shuffled on her feet, and Hayley looked equally unsettled. As Airborne, a consideration of consequences never entered into anything. Life was demonstrably without them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How about you tell us?\'92 Hayley challenged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy was momentarily flustered. \'91Well, I don\'92t know either, obviously, but there has to be some, right. How can stasis tech oppose entropy without some serious voodoo behind it? I mean, any seriously advanced advanced magic must have the Rube Goldberg Machine from Hell behind it, right? Who\'92s to say\'97I dunno\'97there\'92s not one of those horrendous Gyges Discs in every fridge in the land?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92 Sarah demanded while Hayley\'92s blood chilled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sure,\'92 Kathy asserted. \'91Maybe fridges 
\f2\i are
\f1\i0  powered by Heaven and Hell on a biscuit. By the same thing that causes memory losses and time jumps? Maybe this pastrami just keeps forgetting how old it is.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Kathy,\'92 Sarah soothed. \'91Maybe you really should use a patch.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I ran out! When the Hell is Eloise going to bring me my taxing patches?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Look, take a seat, okay?\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Is it so hard to believe, that the one perfect technology isn\'92t so perfect after all? Taxing hell. If it\'92s so benign, why is there a 
\f2\i Refrigeration Secrets Act
\f1\i0 ? An act of parliament, for tax sake.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well that\'92s easy,\'92 Sarah said with a benign sense of world order. \'91It\'92s to protect the Intellectual Property of\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91See?\'92 Kathy demanded. \'91Here we are again, protecting the people who stole the land for farms in the first place. You can\'92t sing the virtues of IP law without acknowledging its darker side.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah was becoming indignant, in spite of herself. \'91Oh? And what\'92s that?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The same laws that protect Intellectual\'97I won\'92t use the \'93P\'94 word again\'97are equally good at covering up Intellectual Stupidity.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah blinked the shock away, while Hayley stood mutely by, her investigative mind processing at speed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Take a look at the world and tell me you don\'92t see stupidity everywhere you look. But somehow this one perfect, benign technology is the exception to all that?\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Kathy, please \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Okay,\'92 Kathy said with wild grin on her face. \'91If you think that fridge is so safe, how about you both spend the night in there, and we\'92ll see how perfect you both look in the morning. Well, not you, Sarah, you always look perfect.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah self-consciously patted her stomach; Hayley brushed her eyebrow straight and hoped her make-up was holding.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What? No takers?\'92 Wide-eyed, Kathy yanked a drawer open, and snatched a knife. \'91I\'92ll prove it to you.\'92 Within a second, she\'92d flung open the status fridge\'92s wide door and marched inside.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley and Sarah followed. \'91What are you doing?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m gonna open up this thing and see how it works.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92 Haley and Sarah competed to look more alarmed than the other.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You know what? What if stasis tech 
\f2\i is
\f1\i0  powered by Gyges Discs? Sure, why not? Call me crazy, but it kinda seems like the antithesis of perfection, doesn\'92t it, the exact opposite of our seemingly perfect stasis tech?\'92 At which point, Kathy launched herself to the fridge. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No. Kathy, no,\'92 Hayley pleaded as she and Sarah grabbed for Kathy\'92s shoulders. Kathy, with the greater head of steam and momentum, shrugged them off easily. \'91You\'92ll get us all arrested,\'92 Hayley said with both authority and a trace of panic in her voice.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy scanned the walls inside the fridge and found a sign reading 
